       negative holds: no profile row exists between the link and the completion
  (c)  an NGO completes with no GitHub identity anywhere near it, and the database refuses
       GitHub import parameters on an NGO completion
  (d)  four service-role backstop negatives: no linked identity, null stats, an empty language
       array, and a handle the account never linked - the last of which no test driving the edge
       function could ever reach, since that path derives the handle from the identity it checks
  (d2) the semantically-empty forms, rulings R1 and R6, at BOTH locations: through the function
       with a stated reason (a tab-only summary, a [null] language array) and as operator by the
       table's NAMED constraints (ARRAY[NULL], ARRAY[''], ARRAY['  '], a tab-only summary, a
       tab-only handle)
  (d3) ruling R4's bridge: a caller passing ONLY the original five named arguments still resolves
       against the migrated function and completes an NGO signup
  (e)  atomicity, demonstrated rather than asserted - a completion forced to fail on its LAST
       write leaves no account, no profile, no acknowledgment
  (f)  Auth reports GitHub enabled, Google still enabled, apple untouched
  (f2) SKIPPED, the expected case: no GITHUB client id is in the environment, because creating
       the OAuth app is a founder-manual step. Not a pass, and the verdict line says so

TWO MEASURED FACTS THAT COST TIME AND ARE WRITTEN INTO THE RECORD RATHER THAN FIXED SILENTLY:

  * A fabricated auth.identities row needs EIGHT columns, not the five the schema marks NOT NULL.
    created_at, updated_at and last_sign_in_at are nullable in the table, but GoTrue reads
    identities into non-pointer Go timestamps, so GET /auth/v1/user answers 500 for that user -
    which resolveCaller correctly reads as no caller at all, and which the edge function then
    correctly reports as 401 authenticate before completing signup. It looks exactly like an
    expired token and its cause is three layers away. Measured directly: 200 before the insert,
    500 after it, and 200 again the moment the three timestamps were filled.
  * The edge runtime holds keep-alive connections to the gateway across a database reset, so the
    first rpc it attempts afterwards fails at the transport layer and the edge function reports a
    502. The runtime is restarted after the reset rather than the run being retried until it
    happens to look green.

The serving directory was checked rather than assumed, and it mattered: this machine was found
serving edge functions from a worktree that had since been DELETED. The process was alive and the
mount answered browser preflights correctly, so nothing looked wrong from outside. A fresh
functions serve was started from this worktree first.

No key material appears in either file. That is designed in - every key is read from
supabase status at run time and printed nowhere, including in failure paths.

