3: -- The second product migration. It lands exactly what three acceptance criteria need â€” AT-001.02,
4: -- .04 and .05 â€” and deliberately nothing else.
5: --
6: -- WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, so a reader does not read the absence as an oversight:
7: --   * no policies on `public.volunteer_profiles`. Row-level security is ENABLED and no policy is
8: --     added, so every client-key read and write is denied â€” the same posture as the four tables the
9: --     first migration created. WHO may read a volunteer's profile is the tenant-isolation
10: --     deliverable's question (AT-001.21 through .24 and .40), and answering it here would be
11: --     building an untested requirement.
12: --   * no unique constraint on `github_handle`. Two accounts linking one GitHub identity is an
13: --     identity-COLLISION policy, and AT-001.08 is retired in the acceptance file with the reason
14: --     "the PRD defines no identity-collision/linking policy; re-add if the PRD ever specifies one".
15: --     A uniqueness rule here would be shipped behaviour no ratified text asks for. (Supabase Auth
16: --     enforces its own uniqueness on `auth.identities` independently of this table.)
17: --   * no unlink guard. `enable_manual_linking = true` in `supabase/config.toml` also opens Auth's
18: --     unlink surface, and nothing here calls it. What should happen if a volunteer unlinks GitHub
19: --     after signup is a PRODUCT question the PRD does not answer; it is filed for its own board
20: --     item rather than guessed at in a migration.
21: --   * no real GitHub import. The stats written below come from `stubGithubStatsFor` in
22: --     `supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts` â€” the stub import fixture the decomposition manifest's
23: --     cross-contract calls for ("stub import fixture until W3"). This migration stores whatever the
24: --     caller imported and refuses the empty forms; it does not fetch anything and neither does
25: --     anything else in this item.
26: 
27: /* ==================================== the emptiness predicate a CHECK expression cannot spell ==== */
28: 
29: -- THIS IS A FUNCTION BECAUSE A CHECK EXPRESSION MAY NOT CONTAIN A SUBQUERY, not because a helper
30: -- looked tidy. "Every entry is a real language" is an ELEMENT-WISE question over an array, and the
31: -- natural way to ask it â€” `not exists (select 1 from unnest(...) where ...)` â€” is exactly the form
32: -- PostgreSQL rejects inside a CHECK. A small IMMUTABLE function is the supported way to put the same
33: -- predicate there, and the constraint below calls it.
34: --
35: -- WHY ELEMENT-WISE AT ALL, and this is the correction of a real gap rather than a refinement:
36: -- `cardinality(ARRAY[NULL]::text[])` is 1 and `cardinality(ARRAY[''])` is 1, so a slot-count alone
37: -- ACCEPTS a language list that contains no language. AT-001.05's "a queued-but-empty import fails
38: -- this test" is a statement about CONTENT, and a row holding one empty slot is empty content wearing
39: -- a non-zero count.
40: --
41: -- `^\s*$` RATHER THAN `btrim(...) <> ''`, for the same class of reason: `btrim`'s default character
42: -- set is the SPACE CHARACTER ONLY, so a tab-only, newline-only or vertical-tab-only value survives
43: -- trimming unchanged and compares unequal to the empty string. `\s` is the POSIX regular-expression
44: -- shorthand for the whitespace class, so `^\s*$` matches the empty string and every all-whitespace
45: -- string alike.
46: --
47: -- SAFE UNDER `set search_path = ''`: every name below â€” `cardinality`, `array_position`, `unnest` â€”
48: -- lives in `pg_catalog`, which PostgreSQL searches implicitly even when the path is empty. There is
49: -- nothing here a search_path could redirect.
50: --
51: -- `array_position(entries, null::text)` FINDS NULLS on purpose: its comparisons use
52: -- `IS NOT DISTINCT FROM` semantics, which is documented and is the reason it can search for NULL at
53: -- all â€” `= null` never could. The cast is explicit because a bare `null` has no type to resolve the
54: -- polymorphic argument against.
55: create function public.text_array_entries_all_populated(entries text[])
56: returns boolean
57: language sql
58: immutable
59: set search_path = ''
60: as $$
61:   select entries is not null
62:      and cardinality(entries) >= 1
63:      and array_position(entries, null::text) is null
64:      and not exists (select 1 from unnest(entries) as entry where entry ~ '^\s*$');
65: $$;
66: 
67: comment on function public.text_array_entries_all_populated(text[]) is
68:   'True when a text array holds at least one entry and no entry is NULL, empty or whitespace-only. It exists because a CHECK expression cannot contain a subquery (REQ-001, AT-001.05).';
69: 
70: -- THE SAME REVOKE EVERY FUNCTION IN THIS SCHEMA GETS, and nothing follows it. PostgreSQL grants
71: -- EXECUTE on a new function to PUBLIC, and every Data API role inherits that, so the revoke is what
72: -- makes the posture true rather than assumed. NO GRANT IS NEEDED: the only INSERT into
73: -- `public.volunteer_profiles` runs inside `public.complete_signup`, whose definer is this schema's
74: -- owner, and an owner needs no privilege on its own function. That distinction is load-bearing here
75: -- because a CHECK expression IS evaluated with the current user's privileges â€” so a different writer
76: -- would need EXECUTE, and there is deliberately no different writer.
77: revoke execute on function public.text_array_entries_all_populated(text[]) from public;
78: 
79: /* ================================================================ the imported profile table ==== */
80: 
81: -- ONE ROW PER VOLUNTEER ACCOUNT, keyed by the account itself. The primary key IS the account id, so
82: -- "a volunteer has at most one imported profile" is structural rather than a rule somebody remembers
83: -- to apply, exactly as `public.accounts`'s primary key makes one-type-per-account structural.
84: --
85: -- THE FOUR CHECK CONSTRAINTS ARE AT-001.05'S LAST SENTENCE AS A SHAPE. The criterion ends "a
86: -- queued-but-empty import fails this test", so the empty forms are refused by the table and not by a
87: -- convention: a blank or whitespace-only handle, a language list with nothing in it OR with nothing
88: -- but NULL and blank slots in it, a negative repository count and a blank or whitespace-only summary
89: -- cannot be stored at all, by any caller, through any path.
90: --
91: -- WHAT "EMPTY" HAD TO BE WIDENED TO MEAN, because the first version of these constraints said the
92: -- sentence above while accepting three inputs that make it false, and a comment the catalog
93: -- contradicts is worse than no comment:
94: --   * `btrim(x) <> ''` trims THE SPACE CHARACTER ONLY. A tab-only handle or summary passed it
95: --     untouched. The scalar constraints are therefore `x !~ '^\s*$'` â€” `\s` is the POSIX
96: --     regular-expression whitespace class, so the empty string and every all-whitespace string are
97: --     refused by one expression.
98: --   * `cardinality(...) >= 1` counts SLOTS, not languages: `ARRAY[NULL]` and `ARRAY['']` both have
99: --     cardinality 1. The languages constraint therefore calls
100: --     `public.text_array_entries_all_populated` above, which asks the element-wise question a CHECK
101: --     expression cannot spell for itself.
102: --
103: -- `cardinality`, AND NOT `array_length(top_languages, 1)`, remains the count inside that helper, and
104: -- the difference is a constraint rather than a stylistic preference. `array_length` returns NULL for
105: -- an empty array â€” an empty array has no dimensions â€” and a CHECK constraint whose expression
106: -- evaluates to NULL PASSES, because SQL's three-valued logic treats NULL as not-false. The
107: -- `array_length` form would therefore have enforced nothing at all on the one input it exists to
108: -- refuse, and nothing downstream would ever have noticed: the stub stats are non-empty by
109: -- construction, so every test would have stayed green while the structural guarantee was a fiction.
110: -- `cardinality` returns 0 for the empty array and never NULL, so the comparison is two-valued on
111: -- exactly the input that matters. The live proof calls this function directly with `'{}'::text[]`,
112: -- and inserts `ARRAY[NULL]::text[]`, `ARRAY['']`, `ARRAY['  ']`, a tab-only handle and a tab-only
113: -- summary as operator, requiring a refusal from each â€” so every clause above is measured on the
114: -- migrated database rather than reasoned about here.
115: --
116: -- `repository_count >= 0` PERMITS ZERO on purpose, and the asymmetry with the stub is deliberate: a
117: -- real import may legitimately find a volunteer with no public repositories, so the column allows
118: -- it. `stubGithubStatsFor` never produces zero, so a zero read back in a test would mean the value
119: -- did not come from the stub.
120: create table public.volunteer_profiles (
121:   account_id uuid primary key references public.accounts (id) on delete cascade,
122:   github_handle text not null constraint volunteer_profiles_github_handle_present check (github_handle !~ '^\s*$'),
123:   top_languages text[] not null constraint volunteer_profiles_top_languages_present check (public.text_array_entries_all_populated(top_languages)),
124:   repository_count integer not null constraint volunteer_profiles_repository_count_sane check (repository_count >= 0),
125:   contribution_summary text not null constraint volunteer_profiles_contribution_summary_present check (contribution_summary !~ '^\s*$'),
126:   imported_at timestamptz not null default now()
127: );
128: 
129: comment on table public.volunteer_profiles is
130:   'The volunteer GitHub onboarding import, written in the same transaction as the account (REQ-001, AT-001.05). The import SOURCE is the stub fixture in supabase/functions/_shared/github.ts until the volunteer-profile requirement lands the real one; the firing and the populated row are real.';
131: 
132: alter table public.volunteer_profiles enable row level security;
133: 
134: /* ====================================================== the write path, recreated with the gate == */
135: 
136: -- THE DROP IS LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness.
137: --
138: -- `create function` with a new signature would OVERLOAD `public.complete_signup` rather than replace
139: -- it, and PostgREST refuses an rpc name it cannot resolve to one function â€” the deployed edge
140: -- function would start failing on a name that had worked for months, with an error about ambiguity
141: -- that says nothing about this migration. Dropping first and recreating is one statement pair in one
142: -- migration, which is one transaction: there is no window in which no `complete_signup` exists.
143: --
144: -- WHAT THE DROP DOES *NOT* GIVE, and an earlier draft of this comment claimed it did.
145: -- `supabase/functions/complete-signup/index.ts` is the sole caller of this function in the tree and
146: -- it is updated in the same commit â€” but SOURCE CO-LOCATION IS NOT DEPLOYMENT ATOMICITY. The
