58: The `repository_count >= 0` constraint is untouched â€” zero is legitimate for a real import and
59: the asymmetry with the stub is documented and deliberate.
60: 
61: ## R2 â€” slice A [2], medium â€” REJECT (risk accepted and recorded)
62: 
63: > "A service-role caller that passes the identity check can forge arbitrary nonempty profile
64: > statistics instead of the declared `stubGithubStatsFor` output."
65: 
66: **True as a capability, rejected as a defect.** The database can enforce the SHAPE of the import
67: (non-empty, R1) and the IDENTITY binding (the gate-1 F4 check), but it cannot enforce the
68: PROVENANCE of statistics computed in the edge function â€” the only way it could would be to mirror
69: `stubGithubStatsFor`'s arithmetic in SQL, which is the second-copy-of-the-rule defect this
70: item's whole design forbids, and which becomes impossible anyway the day the real import (W3)
71: replaces the stub: real statistics are not recomputable by the database either. A caller holding
72: the service-role key IS the deployment's own authority; the threat model this item defends
73: against a direct service-role caller is the volunteer gate (identity link + handle binding),
74: which holds.
75: 
76: The migration already states the honest contract in its own words (lines 21â€“25: "This migration
77: stores whatever the caller imported and refuses the empty forms; it does not fetch anything").
78: What was missing is the same sentence in the plan's green-claims table â€” **plan section 4 gains
79: it, and the merge ruling's "what the green does not claim" list will carry it**: the database
80: does not authenticate the provenance of imported statistics; any service-role caller can commit
81: any shape-valid statistics for a correctly linked handle. No code changes.
82: 
83: ## R3 â€” slice A [3], high â€” ACCEPT
84: 
85: > "The new acceptance tests inject `authUser.githubHandle` directly into
86: > `validateCompleteSignup` and never execute `extractGithubHandle`."
87: 
88: **Accepted.** The fixture's header claims the loop green proves "the decisions in
89: `accounts.ts` and `github.ts`" â€” but `extractGithubHandle` is one of those decisions and no test
90: executes it, so a regression in it (returning null for a linked identity) would reject every
91: linked volunteer at the deployed edge while the suite stayed green. The claim as written is
92: wider than the evidence.
93: 
94: **The fix strengthens the fixture's own philosophy rather than bending it:** the fixture stops
95: pre-narrowing the caller fact. `completeSignup` in `_fixture.ts` constructs the canonical
96: GoTrue `/auth/v1/user` shape from its stored state â€”
97: `{ identities: [{ provider: 'github', identity_data: { user_name: <stored handle> } }] }` (empty
98: list when no identity is linked) â€” and derives the caller fact through the shipped
99: `extractGithubHandle`, exactly as `resolveCaller` does. Storage stays storage; the judgement
100: about which identity counts moves onto the tested path. A regression in the extractor now fails
101: AT-001.02/.04/.05 at loop tier. The REAL response shape (as GoTrue actually serialises it) is
102: additionally exercised by step 6(b), where the deployed edge function parses a live
103: `/auth/v1/user` answer over the fabricated identity row â€” the two evidences compose: loop tier
104: proves the extractor's logic, the live proof proves it against GoTrue's real serialisation.
105: Test bodies do not change.
106: 
107: ## R4 â€” slice A [4], high, verify-first; AND slice B [1], high â€” ONE DEFECT, ACCEPT FIXED DIFFERENTLY
108: 
109: > Slice A: "Dropping the five-argument RPC and replacing it with a nine-argument RPC has no
110: > compatibility bridge, while updating the edge function in the same source change does not make
111: > cross-plane deployment atomic."
112: > Slice B: "The five-argument RPC is removed before a nine-argument-only replacement, with no
113: > backward-compatible release path."
114: 
115: **The defect is real and it is one defect.** Plan risk 2 said the drop "cannot" break deployed
116: callers because the only caller is updated in the same change â€” source co-location is not
117: deployment atomicity, and the reviewer is right that in any environment where the database plane
118: and the function plane roll separately, either ordering breaks completion for the window.
119: 
120: **Fixed differently than the proposed remedy.** The reviewer's verify-first asks to "demonstrate
121: an atomic schema-plus-function deployment, or stage both deployment orders". Staging both orders
122: is rejected as the remedy: no hosted deployment of this system exists â€” the only environment is
123: the local stack, where `db reset` and function serving deploy both planes together â€” so a staged
124: rollout would demonstrate a scenario no current environment can produce, against infrastructure
125: this item would have to invent. What is adopted instead is a REAL bridge, cheaper and stronger:
126: 
127: 1. **The four new parameters get `default null`** in the recreated function. A call carrying only
128:    the original five named arguments then resolves (PostgREST fills defaults on named-argument
129:    calls; there is still exactly one function, so no overload ambiguity â€” the migration's
130:    drop-first reasoning stands). An old-edge NGO completion works against the new schema; an
131:    old-edge volunteer completion fails CLOSED with the stated GitHub-link reason (the old edge
132:    cannot supply the handle â€” and the old schema could not have stored the profile either).
133: 2. **The new edge function omits the four github keys from the rpc body when the judged handle is
134:    null** (i.e. for NGO completions â€” an unlinked volunteer never reaches the rpc). An NGO
135:    completion therefore sends the original five keys and works against EITHER schema version, so
136:    both rollout orders leave NGO signup unaffected end to end.
137: 3. **The honest residual, stated in the migration comment and plan risk 2, which are rewritten:**
138:    during a mixed-plane window, volunteer completion is unavailable â€” fail-closed with a stated
139:    reason under migration-first, a resolution error under function-first. Volunteer completion
140:    requires both planes at the new version; that is inherent to a feature spanning both planes,
141:    not a defect a bridge can remove.
142: 4. **The verify-first obligation is satisfied empirically where it can be:** step 6 gains a probe
143:    calling the migrated function through PostgREST with ONLY the five original named arguments
144:    for an NGO completion, which must succeed â€” the old-caller shape proven against the new
145:    schema on the live stack. That is the half of the staging matrix that is real today; the
146:    other half (old schema, new caller) has no deployable artifact to stage.
147: 
148: ## R5 â€” slice A [5], low, verify-first â€” ACCEPT (verified against the committed capture; fix is a revoke plus a true comment)
149: 
150: > "The claimed no-table-grants/sole-write-path posture is contradicted by the committed replay
151: > capture, which records `TRUNCATE`, `TRIGGER`, and `REFERENCES` privileges for `service_role`,
152: > with no revoke here."
153: 
154: **Accepted â€” and the verify-first half is already verified:** the committed
155: `migration-replay.txt` (lines 81â€“96) records `REFERENCES`, `TRIGGER` and `TRUNCATE` for `anon`,
156: `authenticated` AND `service_role` on `volunteer_profiles`. Supabase's default privileges grant
157: on new tables in `public`; the migration's comment "NO GRANT ON `public.volunteer_profiles` TO
158: ANY ROLE" mistook the absence of a grant statement for the absence of privileges. The practical
159: exploitability is near zero â€” none of the three privileges is reachable through PostgREST's verb
160: surface, `service_role` is not a login role, and `auto_expose_new_tables` is unset â€” but the
161: migration makes a posture CLAIM the catalog contradicts, and TRUNCATE is not subject to
162: row-level security, so the standing privilege is real even if unreachable today.
163: 
164: **The fix:** the migration gains
165: `revoke all on table public.volunteer_profiles from anon, authenticated, service_role;`, the
166: comment is rewritten to state the measured default-privilege reality (the same
167: measure-don't-reason posture the first migration took for function EXECUTE), and the replay
168: capture is re-run: the privilege query must return ZERO rows for those three roles. The
169: `postgres` owner rows remain â€” the owner's privileges are not grants.
170: 
171: **Scope note:** the same query, extended to all five tables for free in the re-capture, will say
172: whether the predecessor's four tables carry the same residue. If they do, that is pre-existing
173: on main â€” it is NOT fixed on this branch; it goes in my completion report for the coordinator to
174: file as its own item.
175: 
176: ## R6 â€” slice B [2], medium â€” ACCEPTED WITHIN R1
177: 
178: > "The `top_languages` checks accept arrays containing only `NULL` or blank elements."
179: 
180: The same gap as R1, located at the function-body line (200) rather than the constraint line
181: (57). R1's fix covers both locations explicitly â€” the constraint via the helper, the body check
182: in the volunteer branch â€” and the probes exercise both (constraint via direct INSERT as
183: operator, body via the function call with a linked identity). No separate disposition.
184: 
185: ## R7 â€” slice B [3], medium â€” ACCEPT
